[{:db/id "q-21034/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer/id #uuid "3de3318e-ac3e-5b9e-bd98-3c3c30362ac0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "46b5cc84-9763-5021-84f4-eb5ec4cfedf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" :answer/id #uuid "3a8ad7ad-42bc-5b39-aa84-d6c8a4355b3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer/id #uuid "370e82fa-244f-565d-bd7b-686c93e1813e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" :answer/id #uuid "0e7ee85f-00db-53c2-8192-7e71a85b178c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "192b540d-114b-5dca-93b2-0011a1a2c50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21034/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer-field/choices ["q-21034/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" "q-21034/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" "q-21034/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" "q-21034/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" "q-21034/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21034" :question/id #uuid "25002641-d57a-5f83-8d24-8844df9e689a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21034" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x-12.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =6(0)+6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-21034/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21034"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79200"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-114418"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-344301/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "2cfdd7cf-fbda-5a4f-b854-4493e97c69f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344301/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fab84f36-7c4f-50fb-9534-d25126e51804" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344301/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-344301/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344301/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "2fe51037-c9a5-5fad-8473-6b41eede31b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344301/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "96e0c8df-137a-52c4-a093-7581f98828ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344301/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-344301/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344301" :question/id #uuid "1ead159f-d5ec-5c5c-a0d2-cbb1f6821732" :question/math-academy-id "q-344301" :question/problem "In **ascending order**,the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)+{e}^{x}(2x-12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22)+{e}^{x}(2x-10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=1,\\,$ $\\,x=4,\\,$ and $\\,x=7,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1-2)(1-6))=5e>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4-2)(4-6))=-4{e}^{4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(7) & ={e}^{7}((7-2)(7-6))=5{e}^{7}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2$ and $x=6$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6,$ we conclude that $x=2$ and $x=6$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=2$ and $x=6$ are inflection points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344301/field-1" "q-344301/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344301"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
